@page "/sign-in"

@using System.Text.Json
@using Yello.Client.Sessions

@inject IStringLocalizer<ClientCopy> Localizer
@inject HttpClient Http
@inject SessionState Session
@inject AntiforgeryTokenSource Antiforgery
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@*
    THE SIGN-IN SURFACE. FR-2, AC1, AC3, AC4, AC6.

    TWO FIELDS, AND NOTHING ELSE. No "remember me" - nothing in any artifact mentions one, and it
    is a Session-lifetime decision wearing a checkbox. No password-reset link either: no FR, CAP or
    journey anywhere names password reset, and adding one invents a requirement, which is the same
    reasoning that kept email verification out of story 1.3.

    ONE ERROR STRING FOR EVERY CAUSE. "Email or password is incorrect." (EXPERIENCE.md:272) covers
    an unknown address and a wrong password alike, and there is no client-side branch that could do
    otherwise - the server answers ONE problem `type` with no `errors` extension, so there is
    nothing here to branch on. Adding a "no account with that address?" nudge would be the client
    half of the enumeration oracle AD-23 exists to close.

    VALIDATION ERRORS TAKE THE HAIRLINE BORDER, NEVER --danger. DESIGN.md:501 lists "use danger for
    validation errors or ordinary refusals" as the Don't, and DESIGN.md:289 and :482 make the spine
    win over any mock - so the sign-in mockup's `border:1px solid var(--danger)` is precisely the
    forbidden move and is not copied. Neither are its 1px borders, its px type or its fixed 330px
    width: all three are superseded by UX-DR2, UX-DR4 and story 1.3's registration surface, which
    dropped the fixed width because it would overflow a 320px viewport before a word was translated.

    SUBMIT-AND-WAIT. EXPERIENCE.md:273 names registration and sign-in as the only two in a product
    with no save button. The in-flight condition is stated in words, resubmission is disabled, and
    completion is announced. No spinner and no whole-surface overlay: the wait is the password
    verification, which is meant to be slow, so the copy says so.

    THE REASON, WHEN THERE IS ONE. A Session that has expired returns the person here with
    ?reason=session-ended, and the reason is announced through the same role="alert" region a failed
    submission uses - which is the only focus pattern the spine sanctions (EXPERIENCE.md:398) and
    the one InlineErrorRegion already implements. EXPERIENCE.md:396-405 enumerates seven remote
    events and session expiry is not among them, so this extends the sanctioned pattern rather than
    inventing a second one. It names the Session and nothing else: no Space, no Membership, no "you
    were removed" - UJ-4's cold path and UX-DR38 both forbid it.

    EVERY STRING HERE SAYS "Account", NEVER "User". Sign-in is unauthenticated, so no Space is
    established, and EXPERIENCE.md:18 is explicit that where no Space is established the correct
    term is Account.
*@

<div class="auth-surface">
    <div class="auth-panel">
        <h1 class="type-dialog-title auth-panel__heading">@Localizer["SignInHeading"]</h1>
        <p class="auth-panel__intro">@Localizer["SignInIntro"]</p>

        @if (_notice is not null)
        {
            @* Keyed on the attempt so each failed submission is a NEW instance - which is what
               re-announces the alert and moves focus back to it. Without the key, a second
               failure would render into the existing region and say nothing at all. The
               session-ended reason shares the region because it is the same event for a keyboard
               user: the surface they were on is gone, and focus has to land somewhere they can
               read. *@
            <InlineErrorRegion @key="_attempt" Heading="@(_notice ?? string.Empty)" Messages="@_messages" />
        }

        @if (_phase == SignInPhase.Submitting)
        {
            @* role="status" is polite: it announces without interrupting, which is right for
               "this is under way". The wait is deliberately long - the password verification is
               meant to be slow, and happens whether or not the address is known - so the copy
               says so in words. No spinner, no progress percentage, and nothing that moves. *@
            <p class="form-status" role="status">@Localizer["SignInInFlight"]</p>
        }

        @if (_phase == SignInPhase.Complete)
        {
            @* Announced politely for the moment before the entry point takes over: a completed
               sign-in navigates to "/", which lands on the first Space the switcher lists (story
               1.7, AC11), and that page's heading takes focus. *@
            <p class="form-status" role="status">
                @Localizer["SignInComplete"]
            </p>
        }

        @if (_phase != SignInPhase.Complete)
        {
            <form @onsubmit="SubmitAsync" @onsubmit:preventDefault>
                <FormField FieldId="email-address"
                           Label="@(Localizer["EmailAddressLabel"].Value)"
                           Message="@MessageFor(SignInFields.EmailAddress)"
                           Invalid="@(MessageFor(SignInFields.EmailAddress) is not null)"
                           InputType="email"
                           AutoComplete="email"
                           Disabled="@IsBusy"
                           @bind-Value="_emailAddress" />

                @* autocomplete="current-password", not "off". UX-DR33's off rule covers Space,
                   Project and Task name inputs only; here WCAG 1.3.5 asks for the token, and it is
                   what lets a password manager fill this rather than guess from the label. *@
                <FormField FieldId="password"
                           Label="@(Localizer["PasswordLabel"].Value)"
                           Message="@MessageFor(SignInFields.Password)"
                           Invalid="@(MessageFor(SignInFields.Password) is not null)"
                           InputType="password"
                           AutoComplete="current-password"
                           Disabled="@IsBusy"
                           @bind-Value="_password" />

                <PrimaryButton Disabled="@IsBusy">@Localizer["SignInSubmitLabel"]</PrimaryButton>
            </form>

            @* Registration stays reachable from here. Without it UJ-1 - the journey this whole
               epic exists for - would be reachable only by typing a path. *@
            <p class="auth-panel__aside">
                <a class="text-link" href="@ClientRoutes.Register">@Localizer["SignInToRegisterLink"]</a>
            </p>
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly JsonSerializerOptions JsonOptions = new(JsonSerializerDefaults.Web);

    private SignInPhase _phase = SignInPhase.Editing;
    private IReadOnlyList<string> _failureCodes = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<string> _messages = [];
    private string? _notice;
    private int _attempt;

    private string _emailAddress = string.Empty;
    private string _password = string.Empty;

    private bool IsBusy => _phase == SignInPhase.Submitting;

    /// <summary>
    /// Why the person is here, when they did not simply arrive. Bound from the query string.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The parameter's NAME is the query key - Blazor binds <c>?reason=</c> to a property called
    /// <c>Reason</c> - and <c>ClientRoutes.ReasonQueryKey</c> is what builds the address at the
    /// other end. Two spellings of one key is a drift risk, so
    /// <c>SessionSeamTests.The_reason_query_key_matches_the_parameter_that_reads_it</c> asserts
    /// they agree rather than leaving it to whoever renames one.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery]
    public string? Reason { get; set; }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        // Read from the address rather than from client state, because the navigation that brings
        // a person here has already destroyed the component tree any state would have lived in -
        // and because it survives the reload they may well perform next.
        if (!string.Equals(Reason, ClientRoutes.SessionEndedReason, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        {
            return;
        }

        // THE COMPONENT INSTANCE IS REUSED, AND EVERY FIELD FROM ITS PREVIOUS LIFE SURVIVES.
        //
        // The router resolves /sign-in?reason=session-ended to this same component type, so Blazor
        // hands it new parameters rather than constructing a fresh one. Two things went wrong
        // because of that, both found at code review on 2026-09-19:
        //
        // _phase was never reset. Someone who signed in on this surface (_phase = Complete) and
        // whose Session then ended was shown the SignInComplete copy and the SessionEnded copy at
        // the same time, with no form, no submit button and no registration link - the whole @if
        // block stays closed on Complete. Only a manual reload recovered.
        //
        // And the reason was suppressed whenever _notice was already set, so a person who had just
        // failed a submission was returned here and told nothing at all about why.
        _phase = SignInPhase.Editing;
        _failureCodes = [];
        _messages = [];
        _notice = Localizer["SessionEnded"].Value;
        _attempt++;
    }

    private async Task SubmitAsync()
    {
        _phase = SignInPhase.Submitting;
        _failureCodes = [];
        _messages = [];
        _notice = null;
        _attempt++;

        try
        {
            // AR-14: every state-changing request carries an anti-forgery token. Fetched on the
            // first submit rather than at boot - see AntiforgeryTokenSource for why - and a failure
            // to get one is reported as the transport problem it is rather than submitted without.
            if (!await Antiforgery.EnsureTokenAsync())
            {
                Fail(Localizer["SubmitFailed"].Value);
                return;
            }

            var request = new SignInRequest(_emailAddress, _password);
            var response = await Http.PostAsJsonAsync(SessionRoutes.Authenticate, request, JsonOptions);

            if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
            {
                Complete();
                return;
            }

            await ShowFailureAsync(response);
        }
        catch (HttpRequestException)
        {
            // The request never reached the server. Reported as a transport problem, which is what
            // it is - and deliberately not as anything about the credentials, which were never seen.
            Fail(Localizer["SubmitFailed"].Value);
        }
        catch (JsonException)
        {
            // The server answered something this client cannot read. Same report.
            Fail(Localizer["SubmitFailed"].Value);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // HttpClient's own 100-second timeout throws TaskCanceledException, which derives from
            // this and from neither of the two above. Story 1.3's registration surface had exactly
            // this gap until code review on 2026-09-10, and this endpoint is deliberately slow.
            Fail(Localizer["SubmitFailed"].Value);
        }
        finally
        {
            // Guarded rather than assigned unconditionally, because the success path has already
            // moved the phase to Complete and returned through here - resetting it to Editing
            // would put the form back on screen underneath the completion message. Story 1.3 found
            // the unguarded version leaves the form dead for good on any escaping exception.
            if (_phase == SignInPhase.Submitting)
            {
                _phase = SignInPhase.Editing;
            }
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Turns a refused response into what the person reads.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Split out of <c>SubmitAsync</c> when the anti-forgery branch was added at code review on
    /// 2026-09-19: S1541 caps cyclomatic complexity at 10 and the combined method reached 12. The
    /// seam is a natural one - everything above it is the request, everything here is the reading of
    /// the answer.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="response">The refused response.</param>
    private async Task ShowFailureAsync(HttpResponseMessage response)
    {
        var problem = await ReadProblemAsync(response);

        // ONE STRING FOR EVERY CAUSE. The 401 carries a single stable `type` and no codes, so this
        // branch cannot say anything about WHY - which is the point of it. The 400 beside it
        // carries codes that depend only on what was typed and never on what is stored.
        if (string.Equals(problem?.Type, ProblemTypes.AuthenticationFailed, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        {
            Fail(Localizer["SignInFailed"].Value);
            return;
        }

        // A REFUSED TOKEN IS RECOVERABLE, AND USED NOT TO BE. The held token is bound to the
        // identity it was minted for and stops being valid in four ordinary ways - see
        // AntiforgeryTokenSource.Invalidate. Nothing dropped it, EnsureTokenAsync returns early
        // whenever one is held, so the same dead token was re-sent on every attempt forever and
        // this fell through to the generic "there was a problem submitting". Dropping it means the
        // next press fetches a fresh one and simply works, which is what the message now asks for.
        // Found at code review on 2026-09-19.
        if (string.Equals(problem?.Type, ProblemTypes.AntiforgeryTokenMissing, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        {
            Antiforgery.Invalidate();
            Fail(Localizer["SubmitRetry"].Value);
            return;
        }

        _failureCodes = problem?.Errors ?? [];
        _messages = _failureCodes.Count > 0
            ? [.. _failureCodes.Select(code => Localizer[code].Value)]
            : [];

        _notice = _failureCodes.Count > 0
            ? Localizer["ErrorHeading"].Value
            : Localizer["SubmitFailed"].Value;
    }

    private void Fail(string message) => _notice = message;

    private void Complete()
    {
        _phase = SignInPhase.Complete;

        // This drops the anti-forgery token as well as recording the Session, and that is required
        // rather than tidy: the token is bound to the identity it was issued to, so the one this
        // request carried was minted while anonymous and would be refused on the next request now
        // that a Session exists. SessionState.SignedIn explains what that cost when it was missing.
        Session.SignedIn();

        // The password has done its one job. Dropping the reference is not a security control -
        // the string is still in the managed heap until it is collected - but keeping it in a live
        // field on a rendered component for no reason is worse.
        _password = string.Empty;

        // Signing in lands somewhere (story 1.7, AC11): the entry point asks the Space switcher and
        // goes to the first Space it lists, or says the Account belongs to none. It replaces this
        // entry, so Back does not return a signed-in person to the sign-in form.
        Navigation.NavigateTo(ClientRoutes.Home, replace: true);
    }

    private static async Task<ProblemResponse?> ReadProblemAsync(HttpResponseMessage response)
    {
        if (response.Content.Headers.ContentType?.MediaType is not "application/problem+json")
        {
            return null;
        }

        return await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<ProblemResponse>(JsonOptions);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The localised message for the first failure concerning a given field, if any.
    /// </summary>
    private string? MessageFor(string field)
    {
        var code = _failureCodes.FirstOrDefault(
            candidate => candidate.StartsWith(field, StringComparison.Ordinal));

        return code is null ? null : Localizer[code].Value;
    }
}
